From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] pinctrl: qcom: lpass-lpi: hand the clocks back before the provider goes

Cycling the audio DSP leaves six messages behind, every time:

    # echo stop  > /sys/class/remoteproc/remoteproc0/state
    clk_unregister: unregistering prepared clock: LPASS_HW_DCODEC
    clk_unregister: unregistering prepared clock: LPASS_HW_MACRO
    WARNING: drivers/clk/clk.c:4482 at clk_core_disable+0x74/0x1a8
    WARNING: drivers/clk/clk.c:4482 at clk_core_unprepare+0x84/0x230
      clk_disable <- pm_clk_suspend <- pm_generic_runtime_suspend <- rpm_suspend
    # echo start > /sys/class/remoteproc/remoteproc0/state
    6e80000.pinctrl: __pm_clk_enable: failed to enable clk ..., error -6
    6e80000.pinctrl: __pm_clk_enable: failed to enable clk ..., error -6

All six are one defect. This pin controller takes its two clocks from a
controller on the audio DSP's packet-router bus --

    lpass_tlmm: pinctrl@6e80000 {
        clocks = <&q6prmcc LPASS_HW_MACRO_VOTE  ...>,
                 <&q6prmcc LPASS_HW_DCODEC_VOTE ...>;
        clock-names = "core", "audio";
    };

-- and that provider is destroyed and recreated every time the DSP restarts.
pm_clk holds its clk_get() references for the lifetime of the driver, so
clk_unregister() runs with them still held and, rather than failing, hands
every one of them clk_nodrv_ops. From then on .prepare and .enable return
-ENXIO for good, which is the -6 above and means this device's vote on the
LPASS block is dead for the rest of the boot; .disable and .unprepare are a
WARN_ON_ONCE(), which the autosuspend following the teardown walks into twice.

Nothing has broken so far only because pm_clk_resume() ignores enable failures
-- it marks the entry PCE_STATUS_ERROR and returns 0 regardless -- and because
the LPASS macro codecs hold the very same two votes on fresh handles. The
pin controller's vote is redundant while any macro is bound. It stops being
redundant the moment they all go away.

Give the clocks back before they are unregistered. BUS_NOTIFY_UNBIND_DRIVER
reaches us from __device_release_driver() before it calls device_remove(), so
the provider's clocks are still live and registered, and pm_clk_destroy()
releases them properly -- __pm_clk_remove() disables, unprepares and puts each
entry according to its status. clk_unregister() then finds no consumer left,
no handle is ever retired under us, and BUS_NOTIFY_BOUND_DRIVER on the
replacement provider gets fresh ones.

Both halves switch runtime PM off across the swap rather than taking a
reference. Taking one would force a resume, and a resume at unbind time means
clk_prepare_enable() on a DSP that has already gone down: it fails and
__pm_clk_enable() reports it, trading these messages for a new one. Disabling
instead turns a racing pm_runtime_resume_and_get() from a pin access into
-EACCES for the few microseconds this takes, and waits for a callback already
in flight, without driving the device through a transition itself.

Deliberately NOT a device link to the provider, which is the obvious
alternative and is what the macro codecs use (they are consumers of the same
controller). It works, and it was tried: it also makes this device unbind, and
unbinding a pin controller force-unbinds everything under its GPIO chip --
including the reset.gpio.* auxiliary devices behind the speakers' reset-gpios,
which never come back, because force-unbound devices do not reach the
deferred-probe list. wsa884x takes that reset with
devm_reset_control_get_optional_shared(), so it probes happily with no reset
controller and never deasserts: all four WSA8845s stay in reset, every device
still reads BOUND, nothing defers and nothing errors. Here no bind state
changes at all.

Platforms whose LPI pin controller takes its clocks from an always-on block
resolve a provider that never unbinds, so the notifier never fires.

Not hibernation-specific: a plain SSR reproduces it, and the hibernate path
merely performs a stop/start.

Revision 2. Revision 1 also called pm_clk_resume() from the bind half, to put
the freshly acquired entries back to PCE_STATUS_ENABLED. That is wrong, and on
hardware it is worse than the problem it was fixing.

These clocks have no .enable/.disable, so clk_is_enabled_when_prepared() is
true, pm_clk defers the clk_prepare() to the first pm_clk_resume() -- and
clk_prepare() here is a synchronous GPR vote to a DSP that has only just
reported up. It is not reliably ready to answer:

    qcom-prm gprsvc:service:2:2: CMD timeout for [100100f] opcode
    6e80000.pinctrl: __pm_clk_enable: failed to enable clk ..., error -110

Those five seconds are spent inside the bus notifier, with this device's
runtime PM disabled and pctrl->lock held, so every pin access stalls behind
them. It fails va_macro's probe outright and delays the PDR ack far enough
that the ADSP crashes on its own servreg_monitor.c:537 timeout and the whole
cycle repeats. One stop/start in four took the fast path and looked clean,
which is exactly how it got through review.

So re-acquire only and never enable. pm_clk_suspend() acts solely on
PCE_STATUS_ENABLED entries, so entries left acquired are balanced whatever
state the device is in, and the vote is taken by the next ordinary runtime
resume -- off the notifier, and on a path that already tolerates failure.

---
--- a/drivers/pinctrl/qcom/pinctrl-lpass-lpi.c
+++ b/drivers/pinctrl/qcom/pinctrl-lpass-lpi.c
@@ -37,6 +37,10 @@
 	struct mutex lock;
 	DECLARE_BITMAP(ever_gpio, MAX_NR_GPIO);
 	const struct lpi_pinctrl_variant_data *data;
+	/* See lpi_pinctrl_clk_notify() */
+	struct notifier_block clk_nb;
+	struct device_node *clk_provider;
+	bool clks_held;
 };
 
 static void __iomem *lpi_gpio_reg(struct lpi_pinctrl *state,
@@ -496,6 +500,193 @@
 	return ret;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Hand our clocks back before the provider unregisters them.
+ *
+ * On the x1e80100 family these come from a clock controller that sits on the
+ * audio DSP's packet-router bus, so the provider is destroyed and recreated
+ * every time that DSP restarts -- an SSR does it, and so does the stop/start a
+ * hibernation cycle performs. pm_clk holds its clk_get() references for the
+ * lifetime of this driver, so clk_unregister() runs with them still held and,
+ * rather than failing, hands every one of them clk_nodrv_ops:
+ *
+ *   clk_unregister: unregistering prepared clock: LPASS_HW_MACRO
+ *
+ * From there .prepare and .enable return -ENXIO for good, so this device's
+ * vote on the LPASS block is dead for the rest of the boot --
+ *
+ *   6e80000.pinctrl: __pm_clk_enable: failed to enable clk ..., error -6
+ *
+ * -- and .disable/.unprepare are a WARN_ON_ONCE(), which the autosuspend
+ * following the teardown walks into twice:
+ *
+ *   WARNING: drivers/clk/clk.c:4482 at clk_core_disable+0x74/0x1a8
+ *   WARNING: drivers/clk/clk.c:4482 at clk_core_unprepare+0x84/0x230
+ *     clk_disable <- pm_clk_suspend <- pm_generic_runtime_suspend <- rpm_suspend
+ *
+ * Nothing has broken so far only because pm_clk_resume() ignores enable
+ * failures -- it marks the entry PCE_STATUS_ERROR and returns 0 regardless --
+ * and because the LPASS macro codecs hold the same votes on fresh handles.
+ * That stops being true the moment they all go away.
+ *
+ * BUS_NOTIFY_UNBIND_DRIVER reaches us from __device_release_driver() before it
+ * calls device_remove(), so the provider's clocks are still live and
+ * registered: pm_clk_destroy() releases them properly, __pm_clk_remove()
+ * disabling, unpreparing and putting each entry according to its status.
+ * clk_unregister() then finds no consumer left, no handle is ever retired
+ * under us, and BUS_NOTIFY_BOUND_DRIVER on the replacement gets us new ones.
+ *
+ * Deliberately NOT a device link to the provider, which is the obvious
+ * alternative and is what the LPASS macro codecs use. That works, but it makes
+ * this device unbind -- and unbinding a pin controller force-unbinds
+ * everything under its GPIO chip, including the reset.gpio.* auxiliary devices
+ * behind the speakers' reset-gpios. Force-unbound devices never reach the
+ * deferred-probe list, so those never come back, and wsa884x takes its reset
+ * with devm_reset_control_get_optional_shared() and so probes happily without
+ * one and never deasserts: silent, permanent loss of the speakers. Here no
+ * bind state changes at all.
+ *
+ * Both halves below shut runtime PM off across the swap rather than taking a
+ * reference. Taking one would force a resume, and a resume at unbind time
+ * means pm_clk_resume() -> clk_prepare_enable() on a DSP that has already gone
+ * down: it fails and __pm_clk_enable() reports it, trading these messages for
+ * a new one. Disabling instead turns a racing pm_runtime_resume_and_get() from
+ * a pin access into -EACCES for the few microseconds this takes, and waits for
+ * a callback already in flight, without driving the device through a
+ * transition itself.
+ */
+static void lpi_pinctrl_put_clks(struct lpi_pinctrl *pctrl)
+{
+	struct device *dev = pctrl->dev;
+
+	guard(mutex)(&pctrl->lock);
+
+	if (!pctrl->clks_held)
+		return;
+
+	pm_runtime_disable(dev);
+
+	/*
+	 * pm_clk_destroy() unwinds each entry by its status: an enabled one is
+	 * disabled, unprepared and put, an acquired one just put. The provider
+	 * has not been removed yet, so every one of those lands on a live
+	 * clock and clk_unregister() will find nothing held.
+	 */
+	pm_clk_destroy(dev);
+	pctrl->clks_held = false;
+
+	pm_runtime_enable(dev);
+}
+
+static void lpi_pinctrl_get_clks(struct lpi_pinctrl *pctrl)
+{
+	struct device *dev = pctrl->dev;
+	int ret;
+
+	guard(mutex)(&pctrl->lock);
+
+	if (pctrl->clks_held)
+		return;
+
+	pm_runtime_disable(dev);
+
+	ret = pm_clk_create(dev);
+	if (ret)
+		goto out;
+
+	ret = of_pm_clk_add_clks(dev);
+	if (ret < 0 && ret != -ENODEV) {
+		pm_clk_destroy(dev);
+		goto out;
+	}
+
+	pctrl->clks_held = true;
+	ret = 0;
+
+	/*
+	 * Re-acquire only, never enable. These clocks have no .enable/.disable,
+	 * so clk_is_enabled_when_prepared() is true and pm_clk defers the
+	 * prepare to the first pm_clk_resume() -- and clk_prepare() on them is a
+	 * synchronous GPR vote to a DSP that has only just come up and is not
+	 * reliably ready to answer. When it is not, that command eats the full
+	 * 5 s AUDIOREACH_CMD_TIMEOUT inside this notifier, with runtime PM
+	 * disabled and pctrl->lock held, stalling every pin access behind it:
+	 * long enough to fail va_macro's probe and to delay the PDR ack until
+	 * the ADSP itself crashes on servreg_monitor.c:537.
+	 *
+	 * Left PCE_STATUS_ACQUIRED the vote is taken by the next ordinary
+	 * runtime resume, off this path and already tolerant of failure, and
+	 * pm_clk_suspend() only ever acts on PCE_STATUS_ENABLED entries, so
+	 * this is balanced whatever state the device is in.
+	 */
+out:
+	if (ret)
+		dev_warn(dev, "no clock vote after the provider returned: %d\n", ret);
+
+	pm_runtime_enable(dev);
+}
+
+static int lpi_pinctrl_clk_notify(struct notifier_block *nb,
+				  unsigned long action, void *data)
+{
+	struct lpi_pinctrl *pctrl = container_of(nb, struct lpi_pinctrl, clk_nb);
+	struct device *dev = data;
+
+	/*
+	 * The provider device is destroyed and recreated rather than merely
+	 * unbound, so match on the device tree node, which outlives both.
+	 */
+	if (dev->of_node != pctrl->clk_provider)
+		return NOTIFY_DONE;
+
+	switch (action) {
+	case BUS_NOTIFY_UNBIND_DRIVER:
+		lpi_pinctrl_put_clks(pctrl);
+		break;
+	case BUS_NOTIFY_BOUND_DRIVER:
+		lpi_pinctrl_get_clks(pctrl);
+		break;
+	}
+
+	return NOTIFY_DONE;
+}
+
+static void lpi_pinctrl_unwatch_clk_provider(void *data)
+{
+	struct lpi_pinctrl *pctrl = data;
+
+	bus_unregister_notifier(&platform_bus_type, &pctrl->clk_nb);
+	of_node_put(pctrl->clk_provider);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Watch whoever provides our first clock. A provider that never goes away
+ * simply never notifies, so this costs nothing on platforms where these clocks
+ * come from an always-on block.
+ */
+static int lpi_pinctrl_watch_clk_provider(struct lpi_pinctrl *pctrl)
+{
+	struct of_phandle_args args;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (of_parse_phandle_with_args(pctrl->dev->of_node, "clocks",
+				       "#clock-cells", 0, &args))
+		return 0;
+
+	pctrl->clk_provider = args.np;
+	pctrl->clk_nb.notifier_call = lpi_pinctrl_clk_notify;
+
+	ret = bus_register_notifier(&platform_bus_type, &pctrl->clk_nb);
+	if (ret) {
+		of_node_put(pctrl->clk_provider);
+		pctrl->clk_provider = NULL;
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	return devm_add_action_or_reset(pctrl->dev,
+					lpi_pinctrl_unwatch_clk_provider, pctrl);
+}
+
 int lpi_pinctrl_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 {
 	const struct lpi_pinctrl_variant_data *data;
@@ -539,6 +730,8 @@
 	if (ret < 0 && ret != -ENODEV)
 		return ret;
 
+	pctrl->clks_held = true;
+
 	pm_runtime_set_autosuspend_delay(dev, 100);
 	pm_runtime_use_autosuspend(dev);
 	ret = devm_pm_runtime_enable(dev);
@@ -578,6 +771,14 @@
 		goto err_pinctrl;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Last: the handlers take pctrl->lock and cycle runtime PM, so nothing
+	 * may be able to call them before the device is fully built.
+	 */
+	ret = lpi_pinctrl_watch_clk_provider(pctrl);
+	if (ret)
+		goto err_pinctrl;
+
 	return 0;
 
 err_pinctrl:
